  <!-- Green buildings (published) — text LEFT, diagrams RIGHT -->
  <div style="margin-bottom: 2.4rem;">
    <div class="research-flex-row" style="display: flex; gap: 1.8rem; align-items: flex-start;">
      <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 0;">
        <h4 class="research-paper-title">Hindrances in Constructing Green Buildings in Bangladesh: A Comprehensive Study</h4>
        <p style="margin: -0.3rem 0 0.7rem;">
          <span style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--global-text-color-light);">7th International Conference on Civil Engineering for Sustainable Development (ICCESD 2024), KUET, Khulna &middot; with Sifat Shahriar</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why it matters.</strong> The construction sector uses up to 40% of global energy, and buildings account for roughly 32% of energy use in Bangladesh.
          Green buildings are a response, yet the country has no specific guideline for them and developers mostly seek foreign (LEED) certification.
          This paper asks which barriers matter most.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Method.</strong> A 10-question survey (Google Forms) was sent to 160 experts and members of the public; <strong>87 responses</strong> came back.
          The answers were grouped into <strong>seven criteria</strong> and ranked with the <strong>Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP)</strong>, using pairwise comparisons on a 1&ndash;9 scale
          in AHP-OS. The comparison was consistent (&lambda;<sub>max</sub> = 7.087, consistency ratio = 0.011, well under the 0.10 limit).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Findings.</strong> Lack of <strong>government involvement</strong> ranked first (25.0%), followed by <strong>ownership of residence</strong> (23.8%, since many people rent)
          and <strong>technical difficulties</strong> (18.7%, including scarce skilled manpower and materials). Willingness to convert and overall expense tied for fourth (10.4% each);
          indifference (6.0%) and satisfaction with current bills (5.7%) mattered least.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Recommendations.</strong> New legislation for green practices in new construction, government certification for developers, yearly awareness programmes and loans for homeowners,
          and green construction in vocational and engineering education.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--global-text-color-light);">
          <em>Published, conference paper 2024 &mdash; <a href="{{ '/assets/pdf/ICCESD2024-green-buildings-bangladesh.pdf' | relative_url }}" target="_blank">full paper (PDF)</a></em>
        </p>
