  <!-- DEM manuscript (under review) — method and role only, no results -->
  <div style="margin-bottom: 1.2rem;">
    <h4 class="research-paper-title">Transmission and Attenuation of Bedding-Plane Anisotropy from Sand to Sand-Concrete Interfaces: A Discrete Element Study</h4>
    <p style="margin: -0.3rem 0 0.7rem;">
      <span style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--global-text-color-light);">Md. Abdullah Al-Fahim, Md. Mahmud Sazzad (supervisor), M. S. M. Shafi &middot; Department of Civil Engineering, RUET</span>
    </p>
    <p style="text-align: justify;">
      Aim: to test how the inherent (bedding-plane) anisotropy of sand is transmitted from bulk sand to a sand&ndash;concrete interface, using matched laboratory direct shear tests and a two-dimensional discrete element model.
    </p>

    <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--global-text-color-light); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;"><strong>What I developed</strong></p>
    <ul style="text-align: justify;">
      <li>A custom two-dimensional DEM engine written from scratch in Python/PyTorch (float64), with GPU acceleration.</li>
      <li>Grain generator for clumped particles: three-disk clumps for sub-rounded river sand and four-disk clumps for angular crushed sand, with sizes drawn from the sieve curve and each grain tilted to the target bedding angle with small random scatter.</li>
      <li>A matched laboratory programme of 48 direct shear tests (ASTM D3080) on the two sands, sand&ndash;sand and sand&ndash;concrete, at four bedding angles and three normal stresses.</li>
      <li>Post-processing for peak friction angle, stress&ndash;strain response and three fabric tensors (contact normals, force-weighted contact normals, grain long-axis orientation).</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--global-text-color-light); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;"><strong>What the code does</strong></p>
    <ul style="text-align: justify;">
      <li><strong>Contact model:</strong> at every grain&ndash;grain and grain&ndash;wall contact, a linear spring&ndash;dashpot acts in the normal direction (push-only, no tension), an elastic&ndash;perfectly plastic spring acts in the tangential direction (capped by Coulomb friction), and an elastic&ndash;perfectly plastic rolling-resistance spring resists relative grain rotation.</li>
      <li><strong>Specimen preparation:</strong> grains are dropped under gravity and settled, then consolidated to the target normal stress by a rigid load plate whose height is adjusted each step from the measured plate force.</li>
      <li><strong>Sand&ndash;sand shear:</strong> the specimen is split into a driven upper raft, a fixed base and a free band around the shear plane, so the shear zone develops from the contact laws alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Sand&ndash;concrete interface:</strong> the concrete surface is modelled as a sawtooth wall scaled to grain size, with contact forces tilted to whichever tooth face a grain rests against; the sand block is then displaced over it in small drive-and-relax cycles.</li>
      <li><strong>Calibrate-once protocol:</strong> friction and rolling-resistance parameters are tuned against experiment at a single bedding angle and then frozen, so every other bedding angle is a blind prediction; each condition is repeated with three random seeds.</li>
      <li><strong>Specimen-size study:</strong> the same model is run at two specimen lengths to test whether the bedding-angle signal survives in direct shear.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--global-text-color-light);">
      <strong>Keywords:</strong> discrete element method; inherent anisotropy; bedding angle; sand-concrete interface; direct shear; interface roughness
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--global-text-color-light);">
      <em>Under review, 2026 &mdash; full text not yet available</em>
    </p>
  </div>
